{"bill":{"id":"119-s-3085","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"3085","title":"Firearm Access During Shutdowns Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-10-30","latestActionDate":"2025-10-30","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Crime and Law Enforcement","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"R000584","cosponsorBioguideIds":["C001047","C001075","C000880","D000618","L000575","H001079","J000312","S001232","R000618","M001244","C001056","B001299"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Firearm Access During Shutdowns Act of 2025 This bill requires various federal agencies to continue certain operations, functions, and services related to firearms during a government shutdown. The bill applies to the Federal Bureau of Investigation's National Instant Criminal Background Check System, including the processing of background checks in support of the operations of the Directorate of Enforcement Programs and Services of the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives (ATF); the ATF's Directorate of Enforcement Programs and Services; the activities of the Department of Commerce's Bureau of Industry and Security relating to firearms and firearm-related products, including activities related to processing of applications for export licenses; and the activities of the Department of State's Directorate of Defense Trade Controls relating to firearms and firearm-related products, including activities related to processing of applications for export licenses. Under the bill, (1) these operations, functions, and services are deemed to relate to an emergency involving the safety of human life or the protection of property; and (2) employees carrying out the operations, functions, and services are deemed to be excepted employees. (Under an exception in the Antideficiency Act, an employee whose duties involve the safety of human life or the protection of property may be required to work during a government shutdown. Employees who are required to work during a government shutdown because they fall under this or other exceptions are known as excepted employees.)","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/3085","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s3085.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:08.498Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-3085","plainSummary":"This bill requires certain federal firearms-related operations to continue during government shutdowns. It designates background-check processing by the FBI, enforcement activities by the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives, and firearm export-license processing by the Commerce and State Departments as essential services. Employees performing these functions would be classified as excepted workers who must report during a shutdown.","industries":[{"industry":"firearms","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects firearm manufacturers and dealers by ensuring background checks and export licenses continue to be processed during shutdowns."},{"industry":"government","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill changes staffing and operational requirements for FBI, ATF, Commerce, and State Department employees during government shutdowns."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"d79008e14bddb5ee04160e16","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:40:16.026Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would require various federal agencies to continue certain operations, functions, and services related to firearms during a government shutdown.","whoItTouches":"People caught up in the criminal law, and victims of crime.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in policing, charges and sentences. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public 